## MAJ

### SAFE-MAJ-001: parse_entry_repo regex backtracks to last em-dash on multi-segment line

**Bug:** scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh parse_entry_repo regex (lines 254–266) uses `r'^###\s+.+\s+—\s+([A-Za-z0-9._-]+(?:/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+)?)@(...)$'`. The `.+` is greedy → re.search backtracks to the RIGHTMOST `\s+—\s+` separator.

**Bypass:** a malformed single-line log header `### TIMESTAMP — jrnb2024/decoy@main — jrnb2024/target@main` matches once (single +### line satisfies exactly-one assertion) but group(1) extracts `jrnb2024/target` via backtracking. If DISPATCH-NOTE declares `**Target:** jrnb2024/target`, target-match passes despite the actual invocation having been against jrnb2024/decoy.

**Fix:** in scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh parse_entry_repo, anchor the timestamp pattern to disallow em-dash backtracking. Replace the `.+` with a character class that excludes em-dash:

```python
# Before (greedy .+ backtracks):
# r'^###\s+.+\s+—\s+([A-Za-z0-9._-]+(?:/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+)?)@([A-Za-z0-9._/-]+)$'
# After (timestamp prefix can't contain em-dash):
r'^###\s+[^—]+—\s+([A-Za-z0-9._-]+(?:/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+)?)@([A-Za-z0-9._/-]+)$'
```

This forces the em-dash separator to be the FIRST one on the line (since `[^—]+` cannot contain em-dash). If the header has multiple em-dash segments, the regex either matches the first repo segment OR fails (ambiguity → no match → exactly-one assertion catches via 0-match).

**Test:** in tests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh add a test case with a single +### line containing two `—` segments (e.g. `### 2026-05-10T12:00:00Z — jrnb2024/decoy@main — jrnb2024/target@main`) and DISPATCH-NOTE Target jrnb2024/target. Assert the result: either exit 1 with target-mismatch (regex matches first segment, jrnb2024/decoy ≠ jrnb2024/target) OR exit 1 with parse-error. Either is acceptable; both close the bypass.

